{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This is the statute of the Torah.</b> One needs to know why He designated this commandment with the name of the totality of the Torah, for He should have said ״זֹאת חֻקָּה״ etc., or ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַטֻּמְאָה״ or ״חֻקַּת הַטַּהֲרָה״, in the manner of what He said (Exodus 12:43) ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַפָּסַח״. And one cannot say that He intended to require purification through the ashes of the red heifer in order to engage in Torah study, for this is not what we find among the Rabbis, may their memory be for a blessing, who said (Berakhot 22a) that on the contrary, words of Torah do not receive tumah; and according to all the reasoning of the Rabbis, may their memory be for a blessing (there), even according to those who are stringent regarding ba'alei keriyan on the grounds that one must engage with awe and fear, etc. — they concede regarding those impure through contact with a dead body that they are permitted to engage in Torah study." "<b>And the matter will be explained</b> based on what they said in the last chapter of Nazir (Nazir 61b), and which Rambam ruled in chapter 1 of the Laws of Tum'at Met, that a Cuthean does not become impure through a dead body — and this is his language: ״that if he touched the dead body or moved it or overshadowed it, it is as though he did not touch it. To what may this be compared? To an animal that touched [it]״ etc., until here. Now, the distinction by which the nation of Israel was elevated above the rest of the nations is through the reception of the Torah; without this, behold, the house of Israel is like all the nations. From this we have tasted the honeycomb of the words of El in what He said, <b>this is the statute of the Torah</b> — meaning, this statute of tumah and the conditions of its purification revolve around the Torah, for through their acceptance of the Torah, the people of Israel became something that the lowly spiritual forces crave to cleave to, so as to make of them a bloc of supernal holiness — in their lives and also in their death. In their lives — in that one who touches a dead body or overshadows it and the like, the tumah of the dead body will cleave to them and will not wish to separate except through a great power that HaShem decreed in the commandment stated regarding the matter of the red heifer; and in their death as well, the tumah multiplies, as they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Bava Metzia 114a), on the verse ״אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת״ etc. — ״Israel impurify through ohel [tent], but the nations do not impurify through ohel.״" "<b>And I have already</b> compared this matter elsewhere to two vessels that were in the householder's possession, one filled with honey and one filled with dung, and he emptied them and took them outside the room. The one that was filled with honey — all the flies and creeping things gather around it; and the one that was filled with dung, although some of the creeping things enter it, it is not comparable to the one with honey. So too, a person of Israel who has died — since he is full of sweet and pleasant holiness, when the soul departs and the body disintegrates, the kelipot gather without end, for they are the forces of tumah that always crave to cleave to holiness in order to benefit from its pleasantness. Therefore he impurifies through ohel, and even a thousand houses that are sealed up — if one is open to its companion, the tumah fills the entire enclosed space. But this is not so regarding one who is not of the seed of Israel, for since he is stripped of holiness, there is no such gathering of tumah except for the lethal portion that is bound to the body. And that which causes all of this is the Torah." "<b>And with this</b> I have found satisfaction of spirit [as to] why when HaShem commanded the Pesach in Egypt, HaShem was insistent on two matters: the first, the law of the uncircumcised, and He commanded to circumcise, as they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Exodus Rabbah 17:3), on the verse ״וָאֶעֱבֹר עָלַיִךְ״ etc. — the blood of Pesach and the blood of circumcision; and the second, regarding the law of a ben neichar [foreigner], as they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Exodus Rabbah ch. 19), on the verse ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ לָכֶם״ etc. — ״withdraw your hands from idolatry,״ for through this they departed from the category of ben neichar. But regarding a third matter, which is tumah, we do not find that HaShem was insistent. And although we find (Pesachim 77a) that the Pesach offering may be brought in tumah when the community is impure, with all this, HaShem could have commanded them before [the offering] to purify themselves from the tumah of a dead body, and to have Moses prepare the ashes of a red heifer — and there is no impediment for HaShem to do His will, may He be blessed. And although one may offer refutations to this, nevertheless according to what we have written there is a fitting and praiseworthy reason, for Israel was not impure through a dead body even though they touched and overshadowed a dead body, for they had not yet received the Torah, and regarding them at that time the law of a convert who converted on the fourteenth [of Nisan] was applied, for whom they slaughter the Pesach offering and there is no concern for tumah that he contracted before he converted. And you will find that they said in Pesachim (Pesachim 92a), and Rambam wrote it in chapter 6 of the Laws of Pesach, and this is his language: ״A convert who converted on the fourteenth and circumcised and immersed — they do not slaughter the Pesach offering on his behalf, a decree lest the following year he be impure through a dead body and immerse in the evening and say, ״Last year too, did I not immerse?״״ etc., until here is his language. From the fact that he gave the reason because of a decree lest the following year [occur such a situation], from this you learn that were it not for this decree, there is no concern for tumah of a dead body; and likewise, Israel when they performed the Pesach of Egypt — there was no law of tumat met in them, and it is the statement of HaShem, ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה.״" "<b>And by way of</b> remez [allusion], it further intends in His saying <b>the statute of the Torah</b>, that if they fulfill this commandment — even though it is a statute without a reason — Scripture accounts it for them as though they fulfilled the entire Torah that HaShem commanded to say, for the fulfillment of a commandment without a reason demonstrates the vindication of the faith and the soul's assent to fulfill all the commandments of the Creator, and this is your sign. And perhaps it was for this reason that HaShem desired that this commandment be transmitted to them by way of a statute." "<b>Which HaShem commanded, saying.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say this statement, for I see that it is HaShem Himself who commands to say, as He said: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה לֵאמֹר דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Further, the language <b>אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה</b> is hidden speech — 'which He commanded' — and the mouth of HaShem is the One speaking, so it should have said: ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ לֵאמֹר״. Further, why was what He said — לֵאמֹר — in the first verse not sufficient, that He repeated a second time <b>לֵאמֹר</b>? Further, one needs to know why He did not precede the statement ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ before the statement ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״, in this manner: ״דַּבֵּר וְגוֹ׳ זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״." "However, inasmuch as all the commandments of the Torah are either rational (שִׂכְלִיּוֹת) or traditional/received (שִׁמְעִיּוֹת): rational — those that reason demands, such as honoring father and mother, theft, robbery, oppression, murder, and the like; traditional — Sabbath, for on it the Creator rested; Festival, for the miracle that was done for us through them; idolatry, not to worship besides our Elohim, for He is the One who brought us out, etc., and similar to these — and this is the revealed reason. And there is no single commandment that does not also contain hidden secrets (סוֹדוֹת נֶעֱלָמִים) revealed to Moses and to a worthy person who acquires the acquisition of Torah through the forty-eight things enumerated in Mishnat Chasidim (Avot ch. 6), for then the secrets of Torah that HaShem revealed to Moses at Sinai are revealed to him, and Moses also revealed to Israel of his generation the hidden secrets and the reasons for the commandments and the foundation of every matter." "And in the matter before us, HaShem commanded him to seal the matter and to tell them the thing as a חֻקָּה (statute) without reason, and this is what He alerted us by saying <b>וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה לֵאמֹר</b> to Israel — meaning, and what is the statement that He commanded him to say to them? <b>זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה</b> — meaning, this commandment is חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה; thus HaShem commanded to say to you. It is as though the verse said: 'which HaShem commanded to say to you' — that is, זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה. And from within these matters you learn that reasoned matters concerning this commandment were indeed stated to Moses; only that what He commanded him to say is [only] חֻקָּה, and by this Israel will not demand of him to tell them the reason for the matter, since HaShem commanded him to tell the matter as a חֻקָּה." "<b>And with this</b> the fine points are resolved. Also resolved is why He did not precede the statement ״דַּבֵּר״ etc. to the statement ״זֹאת חֻקַּת״ etc., for the statement ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ is HaShem's command regarding the commandment that he should say to Israel that HaShem commanded him to tell them this commandment as a חֻקָּה, while the statement ״דַּבֵּר״ comes upon the essential commandment — HaShem's commandment to Israel. And given that Israel would say: behold, one who performs a commandment without knowledge of its reason and its secret — the commandment is accounted like a body without a soul — therefore He concluded by saying <b>דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ פָרָה</b> etc. — meaning, they shall take [it] for that which you know and intend in the matter, since you know the secret of the matter and its wonders, and with this the commandment will be performed completely in deed and in the required thought. And accordingly it is precisely examined that He said <b>וְיִקְחוּ</b> with the addition of a ו at the beginning of the command to them, which intends to say that besides the taking itself — that they take the cow — they shall further add that they intend in its taking toward that which is 'to you' as stated." "<b>And if</b> you say: let that cow of that generation be; [but regarding] the cow that they make in every generation, what of it? It is possible that HaShem did not hide its secret from all of them, [but only] from the generality of Israel; but to the singled-out individuals — who are Aaron and his like — its secrets were transmitted, and they would transmit [them] to the singled-out individuals who come after them. Or it is possible that in every generation they would intend toward what Moses knew. And I found that they said in the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah here), and this is its language: ״וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ״ — to teach that all the cows that were made would be called by Moses' name. And one needs to know why HaShem made a distinction in this commandment from all the commandments, that it should be called by his name. And according to our words there is a correct reason for the matter as I have explained." "<b>Or</b> it may say according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 19:8), who said that the nations of the world taunt Israel, saying: ״What commandment is this?״ etc. Therefore the verse's statement comes here and says <b>This is the statute of the Torah which HaShem commanded, saying</b> — meaning, that Israel should say to the nations when they taunt them and say to them: ״What commandment is this?״ etc., they should say to them: ״A statute HaShem decreed upon us to perform, and we do not question it.״ And according to this it is possible that Moses was permitted to tell them the reason of the commandment and its sod, only that they should not say the reason to the nations when the nations press them with words of taunt: ״What commandment is this and what reason is in it?״" "<b>A red cow, etc.</b> I see that all its signs attest to the strict judgments: ״red״ — for this is a sign of the predominance of the judgments. ״Without blemish״ — in having no black hair, and so they said of blessed memory (Parah 2:5) that two black hairs disqualify it. And not its hairs alone, but even its horns and hoofs must be red, but black ones — and needless to say white ones — disqualify. ״Upon which never came a yoke״ — for the yoke sweetens the judgments, in the sod of their saying (Berakhot 5a) that sufferings scour away all a person's sins, for they are an aspect of the judgments. Also, its burning in fire is an aspect of the judgments, and through the gathering of the judgments in the ash, they will effect a driving away of the impurity that clings to a person from the dead, which too is nothing but an evil chain from the side of the chastisements of judgment." "<b>And I saw</b> in Sifrei Zuta (Sifrei Zuta 19:2), and Rambam ruled so in chapter 1 of the Laws of the Red Cow: one may not take a calf and raise it, as it is stated ״and they shall take, etc., a cow״ — and not a calf — end quote. And according to what we said, that all its deeds are an aspect of judgment, there is a reason in this, for the name ״cow״ (parah) declares the known number corresponding to the number of aspects of judgments, as is known to those who know grace (chen). Therefore HaShem commanded that they acquire it when it already possesses the number of its aspect — the number to which it directs. But if they acquire a calf, they acquire something that does not have the number we need from it; and even though they raise it until it is called a ״cow,״ nevertheless HaShem is particular about the moment of acquisition, so that they acquire something in which the intention is that they are רפ״ה (285) judgments."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {12 "The word חן (grace) here functions as a kabbalistic marker for initiates; the numeric value of פרה (cow) is 285, written in the final clause as רפ״ה, matching the number of judgment-aspects alluded to throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:58.262196211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}